          {
            "commit": "eadb94b71a37ca49cda870544d49fe8893d616b4",
            "subject": "LU-16489 tests: lustre-rsync-test/2c sync last unlinks"
          }
        ],
        "author": {
          "name": "Chris Horn",
          "email": "chorn@ddn.com",
          "date": "2026-08-11 18:03:36.000000000",
          "tz": -300
        },
        "committer": {
          "name": "Chris Horn",
          "email": "chorn@ddn.com",
          "date": "2026-08-15 17:06:10.000000000",
          "tz": -300
        },
        "subject": "LU-20580 o2iblnd: don't sleep in atomic in kiblnd_startup()",
        "message": "LU-20580 o2iblnd: don't sleep in atomic in kiblnd_startup()\n\nkiblnd_startup() queried the net device inside the\nkib_global_lock write section. lnet_get_link_status() takes\nrtnl_lock(), which is a mutex and can sleep. The query therefore\nran with preemption disabled and interrupts off.\n\nOn a kernel without CONFIG_DEBUG_ATOMIC_SLEEP, the uncontended\nmutex fast path does not schedule and reports nothing. With that\noption set, might_sleep() reports every call. When another task\nholds rtnl_mutex,\n__mutex_lock_slowpath() calls schedule_preempt_disabled() and\nthe kernel reports \"BUG: scheduling while atomic\". The system\nreaches this path at boot, when the network subsystem holds\nrtnl_mutex while LNet starts. The node then does not boot.\n\nschedule_debug() then calls preempt_count_set(PREEMPT_DISABLED),\nwhich leaves the count unbalanced.\n\nDrop kib_global_lock before the query, and hold rtnl_lock()\nacross the query and the update instead. The net is on ibd_nets\nbefore the query. The netdev notifier walks that list, and also\nruns under rtnl_lock(). The notifier therefore cannot make the\nquery result stale before the update applies it.\nkiblnd_set_ni_fatal_on() walks ibd_nets, so it still takes\nkib_global_lock. The lock order is rtnl_lock() first.\n\nThe query now calls __dev_get_by_name() and\nlnet_get_link_status_locked(). These functions expect the caller\nto hold rtnl_lock(). __dev_get_by_name() takes no reference, so\nthe code no longer calls dev_put(). The old\ncode called dev_put() even when the lookup returned NULL. Before\nv5.17, dev_put() has no NULL check and faults.\n\nkiblnd_dev_failover() clears the fatal flag from the same query.\nThere, rtnl_lock() covered only lnet_get_link_status(), so the\nlock did not span the update. The flag also changed without\nkib_global_lock. That branch takes the same two locks now, in\nthe same order.\n\nBefore the Fixes commit, the query was already inside the lock.\nThat commit added rtnl_lock() to lnet_get_link_status(), which\nmade the query sleep. A later commit removed the RCU read\nsection that once wrapped the query. The sleep stayed in place.\n\nFixes: aebedbe75007 (\"LU-19707 lnet: fix RTNL assert in LNet on v6.15\")\nTest-Parameters: trivial\nAssisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5\nSigned-off-by: Chris Horn <chorn@ddn.com>\nChange-Id: I19b49b26417da5c66be52f085a875a84b5feb6ed\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
